          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/machine+learning"} machine learning
            %em kernel method
            is effectively the analysis of a data set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            through a choice of a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distance"} distance
            = succeed "-function" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi D
                  %mo ×
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \;\colon\; D \times D \to \mathbb{R}
            on it. The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+kernels"} integral kernels
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi λ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(- \lambda \cdot d(-,-))
            turn out to contain useful information, if chosen correctly.
          %p
            As a special case, if the data set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is a “choice of rankings”, hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutations"} permutations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            elements, distance functions come from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+distance"} graph distance
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cayley+graphs"} Cayley graphs
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kendall+distance"} Kendall distance
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cayley+distance"} Cayley distance
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mallows+kernel"} Mallows kernel
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+group+theory"} geometric group theory
          %h2#details Details
          %p
            A kernel on a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K: X \times X \to \mathbb{R}
            (although complex-valued kernels are also used) which is symmetric and positive definite, in the sense that for any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \geq 1
            and any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi N
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1,..., x_N \in X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{i j} = K(x_i, x_j)
            is positive definite, i.e.,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{i, j} c_i c_j K_{i j} \geq 0
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi N
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1, ..., c_N \in \mathbb{R}
          %p
            This may be reformulated as a mapping
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : X \to H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reproducing+kernel+Hilbert+space"} reproducing kernel Hilbert space
          %p The ‘kernel’ and ‘feature map to Hilbert space’ stories relate to each other as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(x, .) = \phi (x).
          %p
            The ‘reproducing’ aspect of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            means that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle f(.), K(x, .) \rangle = f(x),
          %p and this is continuous. So we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(x, y) = \langle \phi (x), \phi(y) \rangle.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is the span of the set of functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{K(x, .)| x \in X\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in H
            for which a functional is minimised, it takes the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi m
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi i
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = \sum_{i = 1}^m c_i K(x_i, x).
          %p
            Many linear algebra techniques in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            just involve the inner product, and so can be conducted as a form of nonlinear algebra back in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p Kernels characterise the similarity of the input set. But how do they get chosen? Often a Gaussian radial-basis function (RBF) kernel is selected:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi y
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 2
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(x, y) = e^{-\|x - y\|^2 / \sigma^2}.
          %p
            Note that there’s a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bayesianism"} Bayesian
            version of kernel methods which uses Gaussian processes.
          %h2#example Example
          %p
            Consider binary classification where we look to form a classifier which accurately finds the labels in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ±
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\pm 1\}
            of a sample from a distribution over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ±
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times \{\pm 1\}
            on the basis of their
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            values, given a set of labelled training data. The support vector machine approach to this task looks to find the hyperplane in the associated Hilbert space,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi (x_i)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (y_i)
            fall in the same half space. The control for overfitting comes from finding such a hyperplane that classifies the training data accurately with the largest perpendicular distance to the nearest of them (the support vectors).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/machine+learning"} machine learning
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+group+theory"} geometric group theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reproducing+kernel+Hilbert+space"} reproducing kernel Hilbert space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mallows+kernel"}> Mallows kernel
                ,
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kendall+kernel"} Kendall kernel
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cayley+distance+kernel"} Cayley distance kernel
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernel"} Markov kernel
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p Survey and introduction:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Thomas Hofmann,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bernhard+Sch%C3%B6lkopf"} Bernhard Schölkopf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Kernel methods in machine learning
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0701907"} arXiv:math/0701907
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Julien+Mairal"}> Julien Mairal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Philippe+Vert"} Jean-Philippe Vert
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Machine Learning with Kernel Methods
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://members.cbio.mines-paristech.fr/~jvert/svn/kernelcourse/course/2021mva/index.html"} webpage
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://members.cbio.mines-paristech.fr/~jvert/svn/kernelcourse/slides/master2017/master2017.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/MairalVertKernelMethods.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_method"} Kernel method
          %p
            On positive definiteness via
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} Fourier transform
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bochner%27s+theorem"} Bochner's theorem
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenji+Fukumizu"}> Kenji Fukumizu
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bharath+Sriperumbudur"} Bharath Sriperumbudur
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arthur+Gretton"} Arthur Gretton
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bernhard+Sch%C3%B6lkopf"} Bernhard Schölkopf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Characteristic Kernels on Groups and Semigroups
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a{:href => "https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2008/"} NIPS 2008
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.is.mpg.de/publications/5466"} mpg:5466
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.gatsby.ucl.ac.uk/~gretton/papers/FukSriGreSch09.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenji+Fukumizu"}> Kenji Fukumizu
                ,
                %em Theory of Positive Definite Kernel and Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space
                2008 (
                %a{:href => "https://www.ism.ac.jp/~fukumizu/H20_kernel/Kernel_7_theory.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/FukumizuPositiveDefiniteKernels.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Proof that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mallows+kernel"} Mallows kernel
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kendall+kernel"} Kendall kernel
            are
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+definite+bilinear+form"} positive definite
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yunlong+Jiao"}> Yunlong Jiao
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Philippe+Vert"} Jean-Philippe Vert
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Kendall and Mallows Kernels for Permutations
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2017.2719680"} doi:10.1109/TPAMI.2017.2719680
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-01279273/document"} hal:01279273
          %p Discussion of weighted variants:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yunlong+Jiao"}> Yunlong Jiao
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Philippe+Vert"} Jean-Philippe Vert
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Weighted Kendall and High-order Kernels for Permutations
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08526"} arXiv:1802.08526
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/jiao18a.html"} mlr:v80/jiao18a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://hal.inria.fr/hal-01717385"} hal:hal-01717385
          %p
            On Hamming distance kernels via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} topological quantum computation
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandra+Di+Pierro"}> Alessandra Di Pierro
              , Riccardo Mengoni, Rajagopal Nagarajan, David Windridge:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hamming Distance Kernelisation via Topological Quantum Computation
              = succeed "," do
                %em Theory and Practice of Natural Computing. TPNC 2017
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 10687
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-71069-3_21"} doi:10.1007/978-3-319-71069-3_21
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%B4ng+V%C3%A2n+L%C3%AA"}> Hông Vân Lê
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Supervised learning with probabilistic morphisms and kernel mean embeddings
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.06348"} arXiv:2305.06348
          %blockquote
            %p In this paper I propose a generative model of supervised learning that unifies two approaches to supervised learning, using a concept of a correct loss function. Addressing two measurability problems, which have been ignored in statistical learning theory, I propose to use convergence in outer probability to characterize the consistency of a learning algorithm. Building upon these results, I extend a result due to Cucker-Smale, which addresses the learnability of a regression model, to the setting of a conditional probability estimation problem. Additionally, I present a variant of Vapnik-Stefanyuk’s regularization method for solving stochastic ill-posed problems, and using it to prove the generalizability of overparameterized supervised learning models.
          %h3#InTopologicalMachineLearningReferences In topological machine learning
          %p
            On kernel methods applicable to
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+diagrams"} persistence diagrams
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/barcodes"} barcodes
            for making
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+data+analysis"} topological data analysis
            amenable to “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+machine+learning"}> topological
            ”
